# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files
.env*
# …except the template: names and comments, no values, and it must be committed so a new
# variable is documented by the change that adds it.
!.env.example

# `pnpm payment:e2e setup` writes the browser pass's handoff here (#739): relationship ids and a
# live session cookie. It is read by the run and deleted by `teardown`, never committed.
.payment-e2e.json

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts
# data:check build output (generated)
.datacheck/
.migration/

# static HTML template drop (issue #1): not part of the tree, not built, nothing
# imports it. It is ignored so `git status` and `biome check` (vcs.useIgnoreFile)
# agree with AGENTS.md instead of linting 8 MB of someone else's markup.
/lib/quizhub/
# per-card git worktrees (kanban --workspace worktree). They live inside the repo so
# `git worktree list` resolves them, but they are never part of the tree.
.worktrees/


# P3 fixture generator output (gitignored; validated by data:check when present)
data/generated/

# bundle exports (#147). `pnpm bundles export` writes here; the artifact worth
# shipping is committed under data/bundles/, and a default export must not dirty
# the tree.
/exports/

# brand-parity failure artifacts (#171): the full HTML of a render that did not match
# tests/parity/manifest.txt. Written only when a route fails, uploaded by CI on a red
# leg, never committed — the committed half is the manifest and the summaries.
tests/parity/artifacts/

# Hermes profile/board state occasionally lands in a card workspace; never commit it.
.hermes/

# The audience and positioning canon is held outside this repository on purpose (#903): the repo
# is public, and the note is a private copy handoff. The copies stay on disk, untracked,
# and this rule is what stops the next `git add -A` from publishing them.
#
# **Five copies exist on disk and four are byte-identical** (`sha256 283263361742…`, 6,436 B); the
# fifth is a 3,672-byte variant. The rule named three paths, so the other two were one
# `git add -A` away from publishing the same canon through a different directory — the gap was the
# rule's, not the author's (#931, decision A). A rule that lists some of the copies is a rule that
# documents where the leak is; list every copy.
docs/specs/spec-design-page-rel-templates-v2/AUDIENCE-AND-POSITIONING.md
docs/specs/spec-design-page-rel-templates-v2/home/AUDIENCE-AND-POSITIONING.md
docs/specs/spec-design-page-rel-templates-v2/brand-profile/AUDIENCE-AND-POSITIONING.md
docs/specs/spec-design-page-rel-templates/AUDIENCE-AND-POSITIONING.md
docs/specs/spec-as2-style-home-redesign/AUDIENCE-AND-POSITIONING.md
